          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Would changing when I eat help?",
          "answer": "The retrieved material only partly answers this, and it does not clearly say that changing when you eat helps.\n\nThe source is \"Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers\" (general gastroenterology educational summary). Its title covers meal timing, but the passage it returned starts partway through and does not say what it recommends about timing. It does say:\n\n- **Weight:** Losing weight, if your weight is above the healthy range, has the strongest supporting evidence of any lifestyle measure for reflux.\n- **Trigger foods:** Common triggers include coffee, chocolate, alcohol, citrus, tomato, mint, fatty or fried food, spicy food and fizzy drinks. Cutting out all of them has weak evidence and is hard to keep up.\n- **Finding your own triggers:** The usual approach is to work out which foods affect you personally.\n\nThe passage also says that some lifestyle changes do more for people whose symptoms are mainly at night, but the text is cut off before it says which changes.\n\nOther passages I searched, on heart failure, diabetes, blood pressure and COPD, are not about heartburn, so I have not used them here.\n\nA doctor or pharmacist can help you judge whether meal timing is worth trying and how it fits with any treatment you are considering. Keep the warning signs from my earlier message in mind: chest pain, trouble swallowing, vomiting blood, or black stools need prompt medical attention.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
